[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Lazy Loading Modules in Angular"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lazy loading is a design pattern that defers initialization of an object until the point at which it is needed. In Angular, lazy loading modules helps reduce the initial bundle size of your application, leading to faster load times. This tutorial will guide you through the process of implementing lazy loading in an Angular application."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Basic knowledge of Angular CLI and Angular Modules."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js and npm installed on your machine."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Create a New Angular Project"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you don't have an existing Angular project, create a new one using the Angular CLI:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"ng new lazy-loading-example --create-application=true --strict=false"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Navigate to the project directory:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"cd lazy-loading-example"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Create a Feature Module"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new module that will be lazy-loaded. For example, let's create a 'CustomersModule':"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"ng generate module customers --route customers --module app.module"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command does the following:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creates a 'customers' folder with 'customers.module.ts' and 'customers-routing.module.ts'."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creates a 'customers' component."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adds a route to 'customers-routing.module.ts' for the 'customers' path."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configures lazy loading for the 'CustomersModule' in 'app-routing.module.ts'."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Configure Routing for Lazy Loading"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open 'app-routing.module.ts' and verify the route configuration. It should look similar to this:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'customers', loadChildren: () => import('./customers/customers.module').then(m => m.CustomersModule) }
];"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'path: 'customers'' defines the URL path for the module."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'loadChildren' specifies the function that loads the module when the route is activated.  It uses dynamic imports ('import()') to load the 'CustomersModule' only when the user navigates to the '/customers' route."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Create a Component in the Lazy-Loaded Module"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you didn't create a component when generating the module, you can create one now inside the 'customers' folder:"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"ng generate component customers/customer-list"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Make sure to declare this component in the 'CustomersModule'. Open 'customers.module.ts' and verify that 'CustomerListComponent' is declared in the 'declarations' array."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';
import { CustomerListComponent } from './customer-list/customer-list.component';
import { CustomersRoutingModule } from './customers-routing.module';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [CustomerListComponent],
  imports: [CommonModule, CustomersRoutingModule]
})
export class CustomersModule { }"},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 5: Use the Lazy-Loaded Module"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your main application component (e.g., 'app.component.html'), add a link to the lazy-loaded module:"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"<a routerLink="/customers">Customers</a>
<router-outlet></router-outlet>"},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 6: Verify Lazy Loading"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run your Angular application:"},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"ng serve"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open your browser's developer tools (usually by pressing F12) and go to the 'Network' tab.  Navigate to the '/customers' route by clicking the 'Customers' link. You should see a new chunk being loaded in the network tab, which represents the lazy-loaded 'CustomersModule'."},{"order":37,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"By implementing lazy loading, you've reduced the initial load time of your Angular application. This technique is crucial for improving the performance and user experience of large-scale Angular applications. Remember to apply lazy loading to other feature modules in your application to maximize its benefits."}]
